<Layout title="Credits & privacy — Anti Key List" description="The people, artwork, typeface and privacy choices behind Anti Key List.">
  <header class="site-header shell"><a class="wordmark" href="/">ANTI KEY LIST.</a><a class="text-link" href="/">Back to the playground <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></header>
  <main id="main" class="article-page shell">
    <p class="eyebrow">THE PEOPLE & THE SMALL PRINT</p><h1>A little credit.<br />A little clarity.</h1>
    <p>Anti Key List is an open-source character-set proposal from <a href="https://github.com/WhyTrashEarth/ANTIKEYLIST">WhyTrashEarth</a>. It explores how leaving out some look-alike characters might make short codes easier to read and retype. It is a design experiment, not a universal standard or a claim of tested accessibility outcomes.</p>
    <h2>Annie Key, illustrated by Nano</h2><p>The blonde character with heart-shaped glasses is Annie Key. Her artwork was created by <a href="https://vgen.co/Mainlian">Nano (@Mainlian)</a> and supplied by the project owner for this site. The original illustration is preserved. Thank you, Nano, for giving Annie so much personality.</p>
    <h2>The original inspiration</h2><p>The repository dedicates the project to <a href="https://www.twitch.tv/aviituber">Aviituber (Avii)</a> and credits her passion for design and accessibility. That dedication remains part of the project.</p>
    <h2>A typeface with a purpose</h2><p>This site uses <a href="https://www.brailleinstitute.org/freefont/">Atkinson Hyperlegible from Braille Institute</a>. The font files are hosted on this site under the <a href="/fonts/OFL-Atkinson-Hyperlegible.txt">SIL Open Font License</a>. These acknowledgments do not imply an affiliation or endorsement.</p>
    <h2>Your samples stay in your browser</h2><p>The playground runs locally in your browser. It does not send typed samples or generated codes to a server, and does not save them in cookies or local storage. Copy buttons write to your clipboard only when you press them.</p><p>The site includes no analytics, advertising scripts, remote font requests or social embeds. The hosting provider can still receive ordinary request information, such as your IP address, when serving a page. Links to GitHub, VGen, Twitch and Braille Institute take you to those services, which have their own privacy practices.</p>
    <h2>Reusing the project</h2><p>The project’s code and character-set proposal are dedicated to the public domain under <a href="https://github.com/WhyTrashEarth/ANTIKEYLIST/blob/main/LICENSE">CC0 1.0</a>. This does not assign that license to third-party artwork or fonts. Annie’s illustration is credited to Nano; no additional artwork reuse permission is asserted here.</p>
    <h2>Questions, ideas, or a confusing character?</h2><p><a href="https://github.com/WhyTrashEarth/ANTIKEYLIST/issues">Open an issue on GitHub</a>. Include the font and context when reporting a legibility problem, and use made-up examples instead of private codes.</p><a class="button button-dark" href="/">Back to the playground <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
  </main>
</Layout>
